Each is a relatively open interval and may be empty. A line touching the
upper envelope at a tie but nowhere strictly has an empty activity interval
and is not visited by a strict branch.

Lemma 5.1 (Ordered active slopes). For an upper envelope of affine lines
with distinct slopes, suppose one line is uniquely active at x, another at
y > x, and their slopes are a, b. Then a < b.

Proof. The difference of the later line and the earlier line is negative at x
and positive at y. Its slope b − a is therefore positive.

    It follows that the strictly active V -totals increase whenever they change,
and the same holds for W -totals in the increasing variable g. A discrete up-
date can jump across one or several activity intervals, but it cannot return
across a crossed wall. Landing exactly on a wall is excluded by strict certi-
fication; no generic-time assumption is needed.

5.3     Discrete pair count and equality
At branch samples define

               sV (n) = |αn |,     sW (n) = |βn |,       gn = g(tn ),

and the component switch sets

IV = {n ≥ 0 : sV (n + 1) ̸= sV (n)},          IW = {n ≥ 0 : sW (n + 1) ̸= sW (n)}.
                                                                            (5.12)

                                         14
By Lemma 5.1,
                      |IV | ≤ qV − 1,         |IW | ≤ qW − 1.             (5.13)
The discrete ordered-pair statistic is
    Npair = #{n ≥ 0 : (sV (n + 1), sW (n + 1)) ̸= (sV (n), sW (n))}
                                                                          (5.14)
          = |IV ∪ IW |.
Consequently
      Npair ≤ |IV | + |IW | ≤ (qV − 1) + (qW − 1) ≤ dV + dW − 2.          (5.15)
A simultaneous V - and W -switch lies in IV ∩ IW and counts once in (5.14).
Theorem 5.2 (Exact equality criterion). On an existing infinite certified
strict branch,
                      Npair = dV + dW − 2
if and only if all four conditions hold:
  (i) qV = dV and qW = dW ;
 (ii) every V -total class has a nonempty interval IsV containing a sample
      tn ;
(iii) every W -total class has a nonempty interval IsW containing a sample
      gn ; and
 (iv) IV ∩ IW = ∅.
Proof. Suppose equality holds. Every inequality in (5.15) is then an equality.
The final inequality forces qV = dV and qW = dW . Equality in (5.13) forces
each monotone selector sequence to make the maximum possible number
of strict increases; a monotone sequence on qS ordered classes can do so
precisely by visiting every class. At a strict sample, visiting a class means
that the corresponding sample lies in its nonempty strict activity interval.
Finally,
                           |IV ∪ IW | = |IV | + |IW |
is equivalent to disjointness of the two switch sets.
    Conversely, conditions (ii)–(iii) make the two ordered selector sequences
visit all qV and qW classes, so they make qV − 1 and qW − 1 component
changes. Condition (iv) makes their update indices distinct, and condition
(i) replaces the available-class counts by dV , dW . Substitution in (5.14) gives
equality.
   The theorem characterizes equality on a branch that already exists. It
does not construct a branch with a prescribed switch pattern.

                                         15
5.4    Closed stationary tail
After the final pair change, equal-total invariance fixes the actual labels
(α, β). Let N be the first stationary index and put
 c = (|β|−1)α−β,         λ = 1+c·1 = (|α|−1)(|β|−1),               µ = c·u0 . (5.16)
Since every support total is at least 2r, one has λ = (|α| − 1)(|β| − 1) > 1.
Proposition 5.3 (Global-origin affine tail). For every n ≥ N ,
                                tn+1 = λtn + µ                               (5.17)
and
                                  λ n−N − 1
              tn = λ n−N tN + µ             ,        un = u0 + tn 1.         (5.18)
                                     λ−1
Proof. From (5.2)–(5.3),
                  δα,β (un ) = c · (u0 + tn 1) = µ + (λ − 1)tn .
Adding this increment to tn gives (5.17), and summing the affine recurrence
gives (5.18). The vector u0 is the original global seed, not a seed reset at
N.

    If the pair is stationary from n = 0, then
                                          λn − 1
                                 tn = µ          .
                                          λ−1
Nothing here implies a minimal scalar recurrence, a Perron algebraic degree,
entropy, or a higher dynamical-degree formula.


6     Full spans and literal reciprocity
For a typed arrow e = (α, β) → f , define
                                                 Z
                       Ue = spanR {u : (u, w) ∈ Ce→f },
                                                  Z
                     Ve = spanR {Aα u : (u, w) ∈ Ce→f }.
Lemma 6.1 (Full span of a strict homogeneous integer domain). Let
                       C = {z : hj z > 0, 1 ≤ j ≤ N0 }
be defined by finitely many strict homogeneous linear inequalities and contain
a positive integer point z0 = (u0 , w0 ). Then the u-projections of its positive
integer points span Rr .

                                       16
Proof. For 1 ≤ k ≤ r, let Ek = (ek , 0), and define

                  m = min hj z0 > 0,        M = max |hj Ek |.
                        j                         j,k

Choose an integer L > M/m. Homogeneity makes Lz0 a strict positive
integer point. Moreover,

                       hj (Lz0 + Ek ) ≥ Lm − M > 0

for every j, k, and positivity of coordinates is preserved. Thus both Lz0
and Lz0 + Ek lie in C. The difference of their u-projections is ek , so every
coordinate vector lies in the span.

   Every arrow domain in Section 3 is a finite strict homogeneous linear
domain. If it contains an integer seed, Lemma 6.1 gives

                                  Ue = Rr .                              (6.1)

Furthermore,
                        det Aα = (−1)r (1 − |α|) ̸= 0,                   (6.2)
so
                              Ve = Aα Ue = Rr .                          (6.3)
There is therefore no lower-dimensional positive alternative under these hy-
potheses.
    The assignments ξ 7→ Aξ and ξ 7→ Bξ are injective. Indeed, equality
of two such matrices gives 1(ξ − ξ ′ )T = 0, hence ξ = ξ ′ . Combining this
observation with full spans makes the reflected labels literal.

Theorem 6.2 (Finite-word literal reciprocity). Let ek = (αk , βk ), 0 ≤ k <
n, be a finite typed word whose strict homogeneous arrow domains contain
integer seeds. Phase-resolved reflected-inverse reciprocity for every seed and
every prefix holds if and only if, at every edge,

  (i) Rαk ∈ EW and is the unique reflected W -selector;

 (ii) Rβk ∈ EV and is the unique reflected V -selector; and

(iii) every reflected source, carry, target, and transformed membership row
      is strict.




                                       17
Proof. Assume (i)–(iii). If

                         vk = Aαk uk ,          uk+1 = Bβk vk ,

then the literal identities (3.7) give

                  BRαk Ruk = Rvk ,              ARβk Rvk = Ruk+1 .             (6.4)

The reflected carries are reversals of the forward carries. The reflected target
and transformed membership rows advance the typed inverse state to the
next edge. Induction in the W -first, V -second inverse order therefore proves
reciprocity at every phase and prefix.
    Conversely, phase-resolved equality for all integer seeds gives the first
linear identity in (6.4) on Uek and the second on Vek . Equations (6.1)–
(6.3) upgrade them to full matrix identities. Injectivity of the label-to-
matrix maps forces the labels Rαk and Rβk . Equality at the next prefix
also requires the corresponding reflected target and transformed rows, while
phase realization requires the unique selector and carry rows. Thus (i)–(iii)
are necessary.

   Formal identities alone do not establish support availability or any of the
inequalities. The next example shows the failure at the first phase. Take

       a1 = (8, 2, 2),           a2 = (2, 5, 6),             γ = (2, 8, 2),
                                                                               (6.5)
       b1 = (2, 2, 8) = Ra1 ,    b2 = (6, 5, 2) = Ra2 ,

and
                     EV = {a1 , a2 , γ},          EW = {b1 , b2 }.             (6.6)
At u = (1, 10, 1), w = 1,

          (a1 · u, a2 · u, γ · u) = (30, 58, 84),       Aγ u = (83, 74, 83).

But Rγ = γ ∈  / EW , the available reflected scores are (b1 · Ru, b2 · Ru) =
(30, 58), and

                 B2 Ru = (57, 48, 57) ̸= (83, 74, 83) = RAγ u.                 (6.7)

The relevant carries are positive. The mismatch at n = 1 is therefore caused
by the missing literal reflected label, not by a tie or carry failure.




